Title: Meaning of "conditional uncorrelatedness"

Suppose that $(\Omega,F,P)$ is a probability space, $H$ a sub-sigma-algebra and $X$ and $Y$ two random variables. ChatGPT claims that $X$ and $Y$ are said to be conditionally uncorrelated if
\begin{equation}\tag{1}
\operatorname{E}(XY\mid H)=\operatorname{E}(X\mid H)\operatorname{E}(Y\mid H).
\end{equation}
However, I would have expected that the actual standard meaning of "conditionally uncorrelated" is
\begin{equation}\tag{2}
\forall_{A\in H:P(A)>0}\operatorname{E}(XY\mid A)=\operatorname{E}(X\mid A)\operatorname{E}(Y\mid A)
\end{equation}
It seems to me that $(1)$ and $(2)$ are not equivalent and in fact I couldn't even show that at least one property follows from the other.

That being said, assuming $(1)$ would have simplified a proof for me (I have some freedom on how to model the problem that I am working on), but I feel like $(1)$ does not have any intuitive meaning, whereas $(2)$ seems like an assumption that could be justified when modeling a real-world problem.

So, what is the consensus of the mathematical community? Am I right that $(2)$ is a common property, whereas $(1)$ is rather meaningless?

Title: Proving an identity for a semi-orthogonal matrix

$
\def\R#1{{\mathbb R}^{#1}}
\def\T{{\sf T}}
\def\a{\alpha}   \def\b{\beta}
\def\t{\times}   \def\emdash{\,–}
\def\o{{\tt1}}
\def\LR#1{\left(#1\right)}
\def\BR#1{\left[#1\right]}
\def\CR#1{\left\lbrace #1 \right\rbrace}
\def\op#1{\operatorname{#1}}
\def\trace#1{\op{\sf tr}\LR{#1}}
\def\frob#1{\left\| #1 \right\|_F}
\def\q{\quad}      \def\qq{\qquad}
\def\qif{\q\iff\q} \def\qiq{\q\implies\q} \def\where{\q{\sf where}\q}
\def\m#1{\left[\begin{array}{r}#1\end{array}\right]}
\def\mb#1{\left[\begin{array}{c|c}#1\end{array}\right]}
\def\green#1{\color{lime}{#1}}   \def\GLR#1{\green{\LR{#1}}}
\def\red#1{\color{red}{#1}}      \def\RLR#1{\red{\LR{#1}}}
\def\fracLR#1#2{\LR{\frac{#1}{#2}}}
$Let vector $n\in\R 3$ be the unit normal to a tangent plane defined by
the vectors $\CR{a,b}$.

Given the following matrix variables
$$\eqalign{
R &= \mb{a&b&n} \where \;\,R^\T R = I_3 = RR^\T \\
B &= \mb{a&b}   \q\;\;\where \;\,B^\T B = I_2 \ne BB^\T \\
H &= H^\T,\q H^{-1}\!H = I_3 \\
}$$
$$\eqalign{
M &= \LR{H - HB\LR{B^\T HB}^{-1}B^\T H} = M^\T \qq\q\;\; \\
}$$
Multiplication by $B^\T/B$ reveals that $M$ is a rank-one matrix
$$\eqalign{
B^\T M =0,\q MB= 0 \qiq M = \mu\:nn^\T \qiq \mu=n^\T Mn \\
}$$

I'd like to show that
$$\eqalign{
\mu^{-1} \;=\; n^\T H^{-1}n \\
}$$
I've been trying to find an alternate expression for $M \LR{{\rm or}\ \LR{B^\T HB}^{-1}}$ in terms of $H^{-1}$ via clever application of the Sherman-Morrison formula, but I seem to be going in circles. It feels like I'm not taking full advantage of the semi-orthogonal property of $B$.

Any ideas? Thanks.

Title: Prove $\sum_\text{cyc} \sqrt{a^2 + 3ab + 3bc} \geq \sum_\text{cyc} \sqrt{a + 6b}$ for $a, b, c \geq 0$ with $ab + bc + ca = 3$.

I have been struggling with the following problem, which was previously posted here but has received no answers.

Problem. Let $a, b, c$ be nonnegative real numbers satisfying $ab + bc + ca = 3$. Show that
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{a^2 + 3ab + 3bc} + \sqrt{b^2 + 3bc + 3ca} + \sqrt{c^2 + 3ca + 3ab} \geq \sqrt{a + 6b} + \sqrt{b + 6c} + \sqrt{c + 6a}
\end{equation*}
Equality occurs when $a = b = c = 1$ or $(a, b, c) = (3, 1, 0)$ and cyclic permutations.

This inequality is very difficult because it is cyclic and there are three square roots on each side. Moreover the condition $ab + bc + ca = 3$ is not easy to deal with.

I tried to use Karamata's inequality and Ji Chen's lemma but to no avail because for $(a, b, c) = \left(\frac{3}{4}, \frac{2}{5}, \frac{54}{23}\right)$ we have
\begin{equation*}
\sum_\text{cyc} (a^2 + 3ab + 3bc) - \sum_\text{cyc}(a + 6b) = -\frac{52899}{211600} < 0
\end{equation*}

I also tried Hölder and Cauchy-Schwarz but it all seemed extremely complicated. Currently I see no promising approaches.

Ideas and comments are appreciated! Thank you for your help!

Title: How to calculate a type of alternating sums

I recently was trying to evaluate the sum $$
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{n+1}
\frac{9n^2-12n+2}{(3n)(3n-1)(3n-2)}.
$$
I re wrote it via partial fraction decomposition down to $$\frac{\ln(2)}{3}+
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{n+1}
\left(
\frac{1}{3n-1}-\frac{1}{3n-2}
\right).
$$
This makes the question into the difference between alternating sums of the reciprocals of integer coefficient linear functions with the same slope, which I feel like I see over and over again. My question is, for arbitrary integer $k,a, \text{and, } b$ how would one approach solving the sum$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{n+1}
\left(
\frac{1}{kn-a}-\frac{1}{kn-b}
\right)?
$$

Title: Integral with Bessel function and non-integer power

I am wondering if anyone might know a way of calculating this thing:

$$\int_0^{\infty} \frac{J_1(x)}{x^{\alpha}(x^2+a^2)^{1/2}}\mathrm dx$$

where $J_1(x)$ is the Bessel function, $0<\alpha<1$ and $a\in\mathbb R$. Matlab can't find a closed form, and I don't have any real insight, so any input at all would be appreciated in case this is known.

Mathematica can find the following answer in terms of hypergeometric function:

$$\frac{
\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\alpha}{2}\right)
\,{}_1F_2\left(
\frac{1}{2};
\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{1}{2},
\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{3}{2};
\frac{1}{4}
\right)
}{
2^{\alpha+1}
\Gamma\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{3}{2}\right)
}
+
\frac{
\Gamma\left(1-\frac{\alpha}{2}\right)
\Gamma\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}-\frac{1}{2}\right)
\,{}_1F_2\left(
1-\frac{\alpha}{2};
2,
\frac{3}{2}-\frac{\alpha}{2};
\frac{1}{4}
\right)
}{
4\sqrt{\pi}
}$$

but this is not particularly useful, as I am rather hoping for an expression in terms of more elementary functions. Any input appreciated.

Title: Is there any function you can integrate with respect to $\sqrt{dx}$ (using my definition of what that would mean) and get a finite nonzero result?

Messing around trying to build intuition for manipulating differentials, I had the idea to try and raise them to powers in a single integral expression.

Using this specific definition of definite integration from 0 to 1:

$$\int_0^1f(x)dx=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}{f\left({i\over n}\right)n^{-1}}$$

My understanding is that $dx$ represents the $n^{-1}$ term, which should means that if a similar single integral expression with a squared differential were to mean anything, it would be: $$\int_0^1f(x)(dx)^2=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}f\left({i\over n}\right)\left(n^{-1}\right)^2$$

A geometric intuition for this is: rather than the limit of rectangles of decreasing base size touching $f(x)$ in their top left corner, the expression is instead counting the volume of boxes built by extending each of those rectangles into the z-axis by its base length.

For a typical function, like a polynomial or exponential, this will always evaluate to zero. However, you plug the function $g(x)={{1}\over(x-1)^2}$ into the squared-differential-integral, the pole grows at the same rate the $n^{-2}$ term shrinks, and you get
$$\int_0^1g(x)(dx)^2
=
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}
\left({1\over {\left({i\over n}-1\right)^2}}\left(n^{-1}\right)^2\right)=
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}
{1\over {\left(i-n\right)^2}}=
\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}
{1\over m^2}=
{\pi^2\over 6}
$$

I tried to find a similar function where you can take a definite integral with $\sqrt{dx}$, but $h(x)=\sqrt{x\over {x-1}}$ seems to make the whole thing blow up to infinity. Is there any function where the expression below is finite and nonzero? $$\lim_{n \to \infty}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}{h\left({i\over n}\right)\over \sqrt{n}}$$
